lesner】, 【11†Jonas Mueller】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled StudentBench: AI and human tutoring yield equivalent GRE learning gains, by Curtis Northcutt and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【12†View PDF】 【13†HTML (experimental)】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAI Assistance Reduces Persistence and Hurts Independent Performance (https://proceedings.colm.cc/paper_files/paper/2026/hash/c2166d01fe4bcd694aba89f608737678-Abstract-Conference.html)\nciteturn13view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://proceedings.colm.cc/paper_files/paper/2026/hash/c2166d01fe4bcd694aba89f608737678-Abstract-Conference.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 22\nL0: # AI Assistance Reduces Persistence and Hurts Independent Performance\nL1: \nL2: Grace Liu, Brian Christian, Tsvetomira Dumbalska, Michiel A Bakker, Rachit Dubey\nL3: \nL4: 【0†Conference On Language Models 3 pre-proceedings (COLM 2026)】 Conference\nL5: \nL6: 【1†Paper】\nL7: \nL8: BibTeX download is not available in the pre-proceeding\nL9: ## Abstract\nL10: People often optimize for *long-term* goals in collaboration: A mentor or companion doesn't just answer questions, but also scaffolds learning, tracks progress, and prioritizes the other person's growth over immediate results. In contrast, current AI systems are fundamentally *short-sighted* collaborators -- optimized for providing instant and complete responses, without ever saying no (unless for safety reasons).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGenerative AI Without Guardrails Can Harm Learning: Evidence from High School Mathematics (https://hamsabastani.github.io/education_llm.pdf)\nciteturn13view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 68; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6view0\",\"pattern\":\"17%\"}); Total lines: 1745\nL17@P0: Base”) and one with prompts designed to safeguard learning (“GPT Tutor”).\nL18@P0: Consistent with prior work, our results show that having GPT-4 access while\nL19@P0: solving problems significantly improves performance (48% improvement in\nL20@P0-1: 1grades for GPT Base and 127% for GPT Tutor). However, we additionally\nL21@P1: find that when access is subsequently taken away, students actually perform\nL22@P1: worse than those who never had access (17% reduction in grades for GPT\nL59@P3: generative AI can aid or impede learning. Based on mixed findings from prior deployments of\nL60@P3: technologies such as laptops (17) and iPads (18) in educational settings, we naturally expect\nL61@P3: learning outcomes to depend critically on the specific design of the generative AI tool being\nL62@P3: deployed as well as the deployment context (19). One of the key design decisions is the choice\nL63@P3: of prompt, which is a set of instructions for how the tool should respond to user queries. While\nL64@P3: standard prompts ask the tool to assist the user without regard for impact on learning, they can\nL65@P3: be augmented with guardrails designed to facilitate learning.\nL66@P3: In this paper, we study how the design of generative AI tools affects learning in an educa\u0002tional setting.\nL67@P3: In collaboration with a high school in Turkey, we conducted a large-scale randomized con\u0002trolled trial (RCT) evaluating the impact of GPT-4 based tutors on student learning.1 Specif\u0002ically, focusing on mathematics, we study the impact of GPT-4 based tutors on in-class study\nL68@P3: sessions designed to help students review material previously covered in the course. Each study\nL69@P3: session proceeds in two phases. In the first phase, students have the opportunity to solve a num\u0002ber of practice problems. In this phase, students are given access to standard resources (their\nL70@P3: course notes and the course textbook), as well as additional generative AI resources determined\nL71@P3: based on a randomly assigned arm; the arms are: (i) access to a standard chat interface based on\nL72@P3: GPT-4, designed to mimic the widely used ChatGPT tool (called GPT Base), (ii) access to a spe\u0002cialized chat interface built on GPT-4 using guardrails designed based on teacher input (called\nL73@P3: GPT Tutor),2and (iii) no access to generative AI resources. In the second phase, students must\nL74@P3: 1We distinguish ChatGPT, the chat interface, from GPT-4, the underlying language model.\nL75@P3: 2\nL76@P3: In GPT Tutor, GPT-4 is given a prompt including the solution to each problem (to mitigate hallucinations) as\nL77@P3: well as instructions to avoid giving away the entire solution; furthermore, the prompt includes common student\nL78@P3-4: 4complete an exam on their own without access to any resources.\nL79@P4: Our main results are two-fold. First, students in the GPT Tutor (resp., GPT Base) arm per\u0002form 127% (resp., 48%) better on the practice problems compared to students in the control\nL80@P4: arm. This finding is consistent with prior work on the benefits of ChatGPT in improving human\nL81@P4: abilities on a variety of tasks. Second, on the exam, students in the GPT Base arm perform\nL82@P4: statistically significantly worse than students in the control arm by 17%; this negative effect is\nL83@P4: essentially eradicated in the GPT Tutor arm, though we still do not observe a positive effect.\nL84@P4: These results suggest that while access to generative AI can improve performance, it can sub\u0002stantially inhibit learning without appropriate guardrails. Importantly, the detrimental impact of\nL85@P4: GPT Base on learning is of immediate concern since the similar ChatGPT tool is already widely\nL86@P4: used by students outside of class for help with assignments. Furthermore, an analysis of student\nL87@P4: interactions shows that students often use GPT Base as a “crutch” by asking for and copying so\u0002lutions, but they use GPT Tutor in more substantive ways like asking for help or independently\nL88@P4: attempting answers.3 Finally, we find evidence that students do not perceive any reduction in\nL240@P10: arm degraded by 0.054 (out of 1) relative to that of the control arm. In other words, GPT Base\nL241@P10: diminished the average control student’s performance on the unassisted exam by 17%. Student\nL242@P10: performance in the GPT Tutor arm was statistically indistinguishable from that of the control\nL243@P10: arm, and the point estimate was smaller by an order of magnitude (-0.004). The fact that students\nL244@P10: in the GPT Tutor arm performed similarly to students in the control arm on the unassisted exam\nL498@P21: 16. S. Ruan, et al., Machine Learning 113, 3023 (2024).\nL499@P21: 17. J. Cristia, P. Ibarraran, S. Cueto, A. Santiago, E. Sever ´ ´ın, American Economic Journal:\nL500@P21: Applied Economics 9, 295 (2017).\nL501@P21: 18. A. J. Lamb, J. M. Weiner, International Journal of Education in Mathematics, Science and\nL502@P21: Technology 6, 136 (2018).\nL503@P21: 19. M. Escueta, A. J. Nickow, P. Oreopoulos, V. Quan, Journal of Economic Literature 58, 897\nL504@P21: (2020).\nL505@P21: 20. E. L. Bjork, R. A. Bjork, Psychology and the Real World: Essays Illustrating Fundamental\nL613@P29: grades. Our content creation was guided by two primary sources. First, we adhered strictly to\nL614@P29: the syllabus prescribed by the Ministry of Education in Turkey.17 Second, we incorporated sup\u0002plementary materials from two extra books for each grade level, developed by the partner high\nL615@P29: school. These books, not publicly accessible, have been reviewed by the educational board of\nL616@P29: the Ministry of Education in Turkey, ensuring that our materials align with official pedagogical\nL675@P32: Class Enjoyment Score [0-4], Mean (SD) 2.22 (1.08) 2.19 (1.05) 2.23 (1.11) 2.24 (1.08) 0.857 0.929\nL676@P32: Participation Score [0-4], Mean (SD) 2.37 (1.01) 2.33 (1.02) 2.34 (1.00) 2.46 (1.01) 0.286 0.619\nL677@P32: